sections.s2_technical:
Comparable is implemented by the class that wants a default sort order. The class implements `Comparable<T>` and overrides `compareTo(T other)`. This is the NATURAL ordering — used by `Collections.sort(list)`, `TreeSet`, `TreeMap`, `stream().sorted()` with no argument, and `Comparator.naturalOrder()`.

```java
// Trade implements Comparable — natural order by tradeId:
public class Trade implements Comparable<Trade> {
    private String tradeId;
    private BigDecimal amount;

    @Override
    public int compareTo(Trade other) {
        return this.tradeId.compareTo(other.tradeId);  // natural order = by tradeId
    }
}

// Usage — natural order:
Collections.sort(trades);                    // uses compareTo → sorts by tradeId
trades.stream().sorted().toList();           // same — uses compareTo
new TreeSet<>(trades);                       // natural order in sorted set
```

Comparator is defined OUTSIDE the class. You create Comparator instances for different sorting needs without modifying the Trade class. This follows the Strategy Pattern — different Comparators are interchangeable strategies.

```java
// External Comparators — Trade class is NOT modified:
Comparator<Trade> byAmount = Comparator.comparing(Trade::getAmount);
Comparator<Trade> byAmountDesc = Comparator.comparing(Trade::getAmount).reversed();
Comparator<Trade> bySymbolThenAmount = Comparator.comparing(Trade::getSymbol)
    .thenComparing(Trade::getAmount);

// Usage — external order:
trades.sort(byAmount);                         // sort by amount
trades.stream().sorted(byAmountDesc).toList(); // sort by amount descending
new TreeSet<>(bySymbolThenAmount);              // sorted set with custom order
```

`Comparator.naturalOrder()` returns a Comparator that delegates to the element's `compareTo` method. It bridges the Comparable world to the Comparator world — useful when you need a Comparator but want natural ordering.

```java
// naturalOrder: wraps Comparable.compareTo as a Comparator
Comparator<String> natural = Comparator.naturalOrder();
// Equivalent to: (a, b) -> a.compareTo(b)

// Useful in: nullsLast(naturalOrder()) — needs a Comparator, not Comparable
Comparator<Trade> deptSort = Comparator.comparing(Trade::getDepartment,
    Comparator.nullsLast(Comparator.naturalOrder()));
// nullsLast requires a Comparator argument — naturalOrder() provides one

// reverseOrder: reverse of natural ordering
Comparator<String> reversed = Comparator.reverseOrder();
// Equivalent to: (a, b) -> b.compareTo(a)

trades.stream()
    .sorted(Comparator.comparing(Trade::getAmount, Comparator.reverseOrder()))
    .toList();
```

Key differences summarized:

Comparable defines ONE ordering IN the class. The class must implement the interface. It is the default for sorted(), TreeSet, TreeMap. It cannot be changed without modifying the class. Example: String's natural order is alphabetical — you cannot change it.

Comparator defines UNLIMITED orderings OUTSIDE the class. No class modification needed. Passed as a parameter to sort methods. Can be created with lambdas, method references, or the fluent API. Example: sort Strings by length, by reverse alphabetical, by vowel count — all without touching String class.

